  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 225 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nWednesday through Sunday:\n\nPrecipitation is possible over southern WI Wednesday night through\nThanksgiving night. The precip is much more likely over lower\nMichigan and Indiana and eastward. There is a lot of uncertainty \nabout the type and amount, so keep up with the forecast when \nmaking your travel plans for the holiday. \n\nSynopsis:\n\nA high amplitude upper trough will swing across the center of the\ncountry beginning on Wednesday. There will be two mid level\nshortwave troughs (upper lows) that develop during this time: one\nover the Gulf states and the other over the Midwest. All four\noperational models (GFS, ECMWF, Canadian, RRFS) have four\ndifferent ideas of how these upper lows will act as they track \ninto the Northeast states through Thursday night. \n\nThe 12Z GFS shows the southern low strengthening over the Gulf \nstates and keeps the majority of the precip confined to that \nsystem. Southern WI and all of IL could see a brief period of \nlight precip (rain with a few flakes possible) for the first half \nof Thursday. The prior 2-3 runs had a similar scenario with the\nsouthern low dominating, but had higher precip amounts over\nsouthern WI.\n\nThe 12Z ECMWF sort of phases the two upper lows, but the southern\none accelerates into the Northeast while the Midwest one \nundergoes stronger cyclogenesis, retrogrades, and stretches an \ninverted trough back through IL and southern WI to extend the \nprecip chances into Friday. The Midwest upper low is a little \nfarther south with the 12Z run than with the past few runs. \nBecause of this, the precip amounts are lower for southern WI with\nthe latest run.\n\nThe 12Z Canadian model phases over the Midwest to a higher degree\nthan the ECMWF and spins into a full-blown upper low over IL/IN \non Thanksgiving Day. The previous couple of runs had the low\nfarther north toward Lower Michigan. The 12Z run has the highest\nprecip amounts over southern WI now compared to previous run. \n\nThe 12Z RRFS is at the very outer limits of its model range, but\nis showing a weaker and more progressive northern upper low and a\ndeeper southern upper low. The 12Z ENSEMBLES favor the more \ndominant midwest low, but the cluster analysis gives a nod (with \n53% of GEFS members) to a less dominant one.\n\nForecast: \n\nThe forecast (mainly NBM) for Wed eve - Thu nt. has peak\nprecip chances between 30 and 50% (highest southeast) for the late\nWed nt-early Thu morning timeframe. We will have an east wind off\nof Lake Michigan (lake temps in the mid 50s) which will keep at \nleast the lakeshore areas above freezing and therefore all rain as\nthe precip type. Inland areas should be below freezing at the \nsurface early Thu morning (lows in the mid to upper 20s). With \nthe column above the surface expected to be below freezing (not \nfar below in the lowest 10kft), snow or a rain/snow mix will be \npossible. Of course, any snowfall will have a hard time \naccumulating unless the rates are high enough. So right now, with\nour light amounts of precip in the forecast, only trace amounts\nof snow for inland areas and minimal travel impacts are expected\nat this time. If we can get a stronger system over the\nMidwest, precip rates and amounts will be higher in southern WI.\nThe current forecast WILL CHANGE, so keep up with it.\n\nBeyond Friday, we can expect brief and light chances for precip.\nThe main takeaway is that another round of cold air will overspread\nthe Upper Great Lakes early next week (Sun or Mon).\n\nCronce",
